        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "625dc17e-949e-5f57-8288-d6fffbe32920",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Catopsis, Crataegus, Catopsis sessiliflora\n- Taxonomy: Bromeliaceae, Tillandsioideae, Rosaceae\n- Places: Europe, Asia, North America, Northern Hemisphere\n- Topics: botanical classification, plant common names, name etymology\n\nFacts:\n- Catopsis is a genus in the botanical family Bromeliaceae and the subfamily Tillandsioideae.\n- Crataegus is a large genus of shrubs and trees in the family Rosaceae.\n- Crataegus is commonly called hawthorn, thornapple, May-tree, whitethorn, or hawberry.\n- Crataegus is native to temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere in Europe, Asia, and North America.\n- The name Crataegus comes from the Greek words \"kratos\" meaning \"strength\" and \"akis\" meaning \"sharp,\" referring to the thorns of some species.\n- Catopsis sessiliflora is a species in the genus Catopsis.",
